Understanding VLAs in C::title
<i>[David Priver], October 17th, 2026</i>

::raw
  <article>

::links
  C interpreter = https://github.com/drpriver/DrC
Variable Length Arrays (or VLAs) are a somewhat maligned feature
of C added in the C99 standard. For a while I didn't really understand them,
but while working on my [C interpreter], I was reaching the point where
I needed to add this feature. A VLA is an array type whose dimension
is a runtime expression instead of a constant. It looks something like
this:

::pre.C
  int work(int n){
      int buffer[n]; // Not a constant dimension!
      for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
          do_something(&buffer[i]);
      }
      return buffer[n-1];
  }
When I first saw this feature, I assumed it was just syntactic sugar
over a call to <tt>alloca()</tt>. In other words, the above would desugar
into something like:
::pre.C
  int work(int n){
      int *buffer = alloca(n * sizeof *buffer);
      // ...
  }
But that is not at all the case, for several reasons:

1. The bounds of the VLA are set when execution reaches that point.
   Modifying <tt>n</tt> doesn't change the bounds of <tt>buffer</tt>. So you can't just reuse
   <tt>n</tt> when doing <tt>sizeof()</tt>.
2. The result of <tt>alloca()</tt> has function lifetime, but a VLA
   has block lifetime, like a regular array. This means VLAs in different loop
   iterations have different lifetimes. And because the bounds is set when
   execution actually reaches the VLA, you can't just re-use the allocation
   directly. And if you actually did just call <tt>alloca()</tt> each time, you could
   exhaust your stack in a loop.
3. The bounds is allowed to have side-effects!
::pre.C
  int unexpected(int n){
      // has side effects, but is only evaluated once!
      int buffer[printf("%d\n", n)];

      // _Countof buffer = cached result of `printf()`;
      for(size_t i = 0; i < _Countof buffer; i++){
          // ...
      }
      // ...
  }

Another of the oddities of VLAs: they can make <tt>sizeof()</tt> and <tt>typedef</tt> have
side effects.

::pre.C
  void whoa(void){
      // No statements in this function
      // `typedef` is by definition a declaration.
      typedef int w[printf("%s\n", __func__)];
      // and yet...
  }
  int main(){
      whoa(); // prints "whoa"
  }

And function definitions can have side effects in the parameters!

::pre.C
  void what(int n, int x[printf("%d\n", n)]){
    // empty body...
  }
  int main(){
      what(4, 0); // 4
      what(2, 0); // 2
  }
The last one is kind of hard to understand. Where is the <tt>printf()</tt> evaluated?
How is <tt>n</tt> visible?

To understand this, you kind of need to understand pre-ANSI C. Once upon a time,
functions were defined as thus:
::pre.C
  add(a, b) // return-type defaults to int
    int a; // parameter scope
    int b;
    {
      return a + b;
    }
Newer versions of the standard have removed support for this old-fashioned way
of defining functions, but the way that the scope of parameters was defined was
sort of rooted in this old model. When you parse the parameter list, those
params go into an implicit parameter scope, like with those K&R functions.  So
this means that in our <tt>what()</tt> function up above, <tt>n</tt> has
already been declared by the time we get to <tt>x</tt> and we have a scope to
put our side-effecting <tt>printf()</tt> call into: the parameter scope. so
that's where it goes.

Oh and by the way, despite being declared as a VLA and having a side effect
when you call this function, the type of <tt>x</tt> is still adjusted to a
pointer, just like a normal array parameter. So we have a side effect
expression that is used to determine a bounds, but which is not used and just
leaves the <tt>printf()</tt> behind.

Oh and even more fun, <tt>printf()</tt> can return <tt>-1</tt> and a negative
VLA bounds is undefined behavior, so IO errors can cause undefined behavior in
your parameter list!

The Realization::md
  At this point I realized I was dealing with an entirely different beast than
  just sugar for <tt>alloca()</tt> and I couldn't trivially rewrite their
  declarations in such a way. I had also recently implemented the
  <tt>_Defer</tt> proposal for C2Y in my interpreter, which gave me the missing
  piece of the puzzle: I could use the same cleanup mechanics I had for
  <tt>_Defer</tt> for deallocating the VLA's buffer.

  I won't go into all of the rules for VLAs (read the draft standard), but
  in my compiler's design I could desugar a VLA usage like:

  ::pre.C
    int frobnicate(...){
      // ...
      if(cond){
        typedef int A[printf("%d\n", 4)];
        int x = printf("sizeof(A) = %zu\n", sizeof(A));
        A buff;
        frizzle(buff, _Countof buff);
      }
      // ...
    }
  Into something like:
  ::pre.C
    int frobnicate(...){
      // ...
      if(cond){
         size_t _A_countof = printf("%d\n", 4);
         int x = printf("sizeof(A) = %zu\n",
                        sizeof(int)*_A_countof);
         _Stack_mark _mark = __save_stack();
         int *_buff_backing = alloca(sizeof(int)*_A_countof);
         _Defer __restore_stack(_mark);
         frizzle(_buff_backing, _A_countof);
      }
      // ...
    }
  And this would have the correct semantics:
  1. Bounds is evaluated once and cached in a hidden local variable.
  2. Bounds evaluates any side effects exactly once.
  3. If somehow we enter this code again, the bounds gets re-evalauted as normal.
  4. <tt>_Defer</tt> cleanup with stack save/restore gives us the correct block
     lifetime semantics and ensures the storage gets recycled if we
     <tt>goto</tt> or whatever other control flow.

Conclusion::md
  VLAs are weird, but with a bit of creativity we can figure out a reasonable
  runtime representation of them that doesn't break a normal C execution model.
  I still personally avoid them due to portability concerns (I doubt MSVC will
  ever support them, but who knows, maybe they'll read this post and realize
  it's not that hard to support), but if you want to use them in [DrC], now you
  can!

